WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Film Courage: Do you have any&nbsp;
practical exercises that writers can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
for defining or redefining their characters?

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.960
Christopher Riley, Screenwriter/Author/Instructor:&nbsp;
I do actually so in in the book we approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:20.560
practical exercises in a variety of different ways&nbsp;
so the first set of practical exercises has to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:32.160
with walking writers through a discovery of their&nbsp;
own defining moments and that can be that can be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:40.720
pretty emotional thing to do to go back and and&nbsp;
really touch those moments that have shaped us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:49.200
in wonderful ways and terrible ways and&nbsp;
then we also provide a set of exercises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:56.800
that walk a writer through discovering&nbsp;
their character's defining moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:06.720
and those are very kind of practical here's&nbsp;
the thing to do now set this much time on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:15.120
you know on the timer and start writing and&nbsp;
keep writing until the timer goes off I'm a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:26.080
kind of a believer in what Anne Lamott talks about&nbsp;
small assignments and in her book Bird By Bird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.760
writing can be really daunting&nbsp;
because it's like running a marathon

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:43.280
it's just so much you can't imagine how much&nbsp;
it is and so starting can be really really hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.640
every day when I start writing it's&nbsp;
still that's the hardest thing I do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.680
requires the most discipline and once I've&nbsp;
tipped over into writing then I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.520
but it's like taking that first plunge into the&nbsp;
cold swimming pool in the morning you just kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:08.320
have to give yourself a push in the back and these&nbsp;
exercises are designed to say all right blank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:18.160
sheet of paper pick up a pen here's your prompt&nbsp;
now go I find that when writers do that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.920
end up writing things they didn't mean to write&nbsp;
they uncover things they didn't know were there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:37.200
and I really love it when we just start writing&nbsp;
and require ourselves to keep the pen moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.760
now this is a very physical way to approach it&nbsp;
apart from a keyboard I think somehow there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:52.960
some value in actually writing with a pen&nbsp;
or pencil and paper and so a prompt might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:00.800
you know think of your character think&nbsp;
of how they need to grow and change now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.280
why have they not grown&nbsp;
and changed in that way yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:13.440
or think of the way your characters say&nbsp;
your character's strength or their genius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.760
when was the first time they realized&nbsp;
that they had that strength or genius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:29.040
write about that for five minutes and so you&nbsp;
might start by writing I'm not even sure like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:38.880
he knows how good he is at the trombone and I'm&nbsp;
not sure why he doesn't know or why he's playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.680
the trombone in the first place and then you&nbsp;
eventually if you just keep writing you stumble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:53.760
upon something that you didn't know you were going&nbsp;
to stumble upon and what is it about the trombone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.560
that is just perfect for his personality what's&nbsp;
happening when he's playing the trombone what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:13.840
is he feeling and that's a really terrible&nbsp;
example but it it shows how just doing exercises

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:23.280
allows you to start finding these little&nbsp;
starters that will lead to bigger things

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.960
one of the chapters I think we start&nbsp;
with a sentence characters start at zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.960
meaning as the storyteller I don't know anything&nbsp;
about the character when they first pop into my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:46.560
mind nor does the audience know anything about&nbsp;
a character just before they step on stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:54.000
and I will only know what I know about a&nbsp;
character through what I discover about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.720
them and the audience will only know about&nbsp;
a character through what I reveal about them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:12.320
so I have to somehow get myself to engage with&nbsp;
this discovery process and once I've mounded up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:20.640
all of this raw material then I'm ready to start&nbsp;
writing and doling that out in a strategic way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:30.720
as I tell the story these exercises are designed&nbsp;
to give writers and actors and directors practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:39.120
thinking about and working with characters in this&nbsp;
way so that all of us can make the discoveries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.680
that are going to make for rich and lifelike&nbsp;
characters who can transform before our eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.560
you know when you mentioned Anne&nbsp;
Lamott's Bird By Bird I believe the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.560
if I remember correctly is based on a conversation&nbsp;
she had with a grandfather or uncle where “You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:02.560
just take it a little bit of a time, bird by&nbsp;
bird.” Yes I think she had to write a report about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:07.680
birds and she was looking out the window&nbsp;
maybe at a flock of birds and she was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:13.600
overwhelmed how am I going to do this I think&nbsp;
her dad was a professional writer or professor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:19.840
and he said “Well Anne just take it bird by bird”&nbsp;
and I think that's just some of the best advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:27.360
for any writer how can an actor use defining&nbsp;
moments to build their character Kathy and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:35.200
interviewed several actors as we were writing this&nbsp;
book because we really wanted to understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.600
how these ideas could be useful to actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:48.640
we talked to two-time Emmy winner tony hale and&nbsp;
talked to him about this and he said you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:58.160
I have found that when I will dig into my own&nbsp;
moments that have shaped me and defined me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:06.800
the performance changes in a way that is&nbsp;
appreciable he had just come from recording&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:15.920
a voice for an animated project and he said and I&nbsp;
did that I tapped into something that was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:23.680
real and deep for me and you could just tell the&nbsp;
energy in the booth changed when I did that and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:33.200
said I don't know why I don't do that all the time&nbsp;
I suppose it's costly emotionally to do that but

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.240
he absolutely recognized that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:47.600
by thinking about the single character&nbsp;
that he's embodying and portraying

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.080
seeking to understand what are the moments that&nbsp;
really have shaped and define this character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.040 --> 00:08:01.520
how do those moments connect with my&nbsp;
own life experience he's able to infuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:12.320
greater depth and complexity into those&nbsp;
characters and he said when he auditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:21.760
he seeks to bring himself in that way to&nbsp;
the role and he says I you know I may not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:28.560
when I audition for that role may not be&nbsp;
the tallest or the this or that that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:35.440
looking for but I can guarantee that I will have&nbsp;
delivered an audition that nobody else delivered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:43.920
because they can't they don't have access to&nbsp;
his inner life only he does that is the unique

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:52.240
gold that he can mine and bring forward&nbsp;
in auditions and performances and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.080
I think any actor can benefit from that

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:05.280
and what I think is so useful for me&nbsp;
as a writer and for actors is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:10.960
we're talking about a limited number of things&nbsp;
that we need to discover about the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.920
we don't have to know everything&nbsp;
about every year of their lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:23.600
we just need to locate those moments that have&nbsp;
most shaped them and it gives us this deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:30.480
understanding that gives all of us access in an&nbsp;
empathetic way to the humanity of that character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:37.520
but possibly the actor could have a history&nbsp;
in their back pocket and it might lend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:45.920
a heaviness here a relief here yeah exactly&nbsp;
and and that's that's for me as a as a writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:52.720
a really exciting thing I'm collaborating with an&nbsp;
actor to create this character I do my part first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:10:00.160
and then I pass the baton to the actor and&nbsp;
the director and they bring their resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:07.840
which are different from my resources and when&nbsp;
it all works the way we dream it will work then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:13.760
you get this really rich character who does&nbsp;
things that the writer never even imagined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:22.320
and the actor has got to hope that&nbsp;
the writer put on the page enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:30.480
for them to work with and then they can&nbsp;
pick up that baton and carry it further.

